// src/handle_check.pdx — umount.pdxfs.M2-002 (issue #5)
// KIND_VOLUME.VOL_OP_QUERY_HANDLE_COUNT wiring + IN_USE refusal.
//
// design/tooling/volume-tooling-ux.md §5.3: before calling sys_umount,
// the tool queries the resolved volume's open-handle count. If nonzero
// AND `--force` is not set, the tool refuses (emits `PdxFsUnmountRecord
// @0.1` with `result_code: IN_USE`, exit 5) rather than tearing down a
// volume something still has open.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why this file is a HARD stub, not a real cap-invoke — TWO gaps, not one
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The design doc's own §5.3 prose already flags one gap: "queries the
// volume for open handle count (KIND_VOLUME.VOL_OP_QUERY_HANDLE_COUNT,
// a new op added by R53.M2 to R52's KIND_VOLUME op set)". A direct grep
// of the landed kernel confirms this op has NOT landed:
// `src/kernel/core/cap/kind_volume.pdx` defines `VOL_OP_QUERY_DEVICE_
// SLOT`(0) through `VOL_OP_DEBUG_PRINT`(9), with `VOL_OP_MAX = 9` —
// there is no ordinal 10 (or any other) named `VOL_OP_QUERY_HANDLE_
// COUNT`, and `cap_handler_volume`'s own dispatch (`cmp rbp,9; ja
// vol_hn_unsup`) treats any op past 9 as unsupported. This mirrors
// `umount.pdxfs`'s own M1 `caps.decl` note (repeated in `design/
// architecture.md` §7), so it is not a new finding — this file is where
// it was expected to bite.
//
// A SECOND, independent gap makes the query unreachable even if the op
// existed: `cap_handler_volume` resolves EVERYTHING it needs (rights,
// row_id) from the CAP DESCRIPTOR the caller already holds a slot
// for — the caller must already hold a `KIND_VOLUME` cap slot number to
// invoke ANY op on it. But the real, frozen `_mount_table[]` per-slot
// layout this tool can read via `libpdx-volume.mount_table_snapshot`
// (see `src/target_resolve.pdx`'s own module header for the full
// decode) carries exactly five fields — `mountpoint_vnode_idx`,
// `root_vnode_idx`, `backend_type`, `flags`, `_reserved` — and NONE of
// them is a cap-slot reference. There is no landed way to go from a
// resolved `mount_id` (a `_mount_table[]` slot index) to the `KIND_
// VOLUME` cap slot that volume was mounted through. **Flagged for
// main**, alongside the two related gaps `src/target_resolve.pdx` and
// `src/unmount_op.pdx` document.
//
// Given both gaps, `handle_check_query` below performs NO cap-invoke at
// all — issuing one against a cap slot this tool has no way to derive
// would either invoke an arbitrary, wrong slot or (if slot 0 happened
// to be passed) risk operating on an unrelated capability. It is a
// pure, deterministic stub returning 0 (never in use), matching this
// issue's own instruction: "STUB the query returning 0 (never in
// use)... The refusal check still runs — just never fires."
// `handle_check_refuse` below performs the REAL refusal *logic*
// (count > 0 AND !force) against that stubbed count, so the gate is
// live code, ready to refuse correctly the day either gap above closes.
//
// A future real body (once VOL_OP_QUERY_HANDLE_COUNT lands AND a
// mount_id -> KIND_VOLUME cap-slot resolution path exists) would
// replace `handle_check_query`'s body with: resolve the cap slot for
// `mount_id`, then `cap_invoke(slot, 10)` (op 10 being the first free
// ordinal past `VOL_OP_MAX`=9) via SC+ ID 4, and return its rax
// verbatim (cap_handler_volume's query arms return the field value
// directly per `kind_volume.pdx`'s own dispatch).
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Encoder-gap discipline
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// No `add reg, [mem]` anywhere. No `test` mnemonic; every zero-check is
// `cmp reg, 0`. Reserved-label discipline: every branch label in this
// file is prefixed `umnt_hc_`.
//
// Upstream: design/tooling/volume-tooling-ux.md §5.3 (paideia-os);
// src/kernel/core/cap/kind_volume.pdx (VOL_OP_* catalog, VOL_OP_MAX=9,
// cap_handler_volume's op-arg encoding); src/target_resolve.pdx (this
// repo, the sibling per-slot-layout gap write-up).

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// umount.pdxfs.M3-001 (#7) addition: lazy_bit joins force_bit as a
// second override input
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// M2's `handle_check_refuse(mount_id, force_bit, out_handle_count_ptr)`
// only let `--force` bypass a nonzero handle count. `--lazy` (§5.4)
// needs the SAME bypass for a different downstream reason: a lazy
// unmount does not refuse at all -- it proceeds and defers the actual
// teardown (`unmount_op.pdx`'s new UO_RC_DEFERRED arm, umount.pdxfs.
// M3-001) rather than tearing down immediately the way `--force` does.
// This file's job is only "should main.pdx refuse before ever calling
// unmount_op" -- which of {immediate-forced-teardown, deferred-wait}
// applies is `unmount_op.pdx`'s own decision, made from the SAME
// `(handle_count, umount_flags)` inputs it already receives. So
// `handle_check_refuse` grows a `lazy_bit` parameter that bypasses the
// refusal exactly like `force_bit` already does; the two flags are not
// otherwise conflated.
//
// The refusal ARITHMETIC itself (`handle_count > 0 AND !force AND
// !lazy` -> REFUSE) is split out into its own pure leaf,
// `handle_check_decide`, so a test driver (`tests/test_in_use.pdx`,
// umount.pdxfs.M4-002 #12) can exercise the real decision logic with an
// explicit, caller-chosen `handle_count` -- `handle_check_query`'s own
// hard stub (module header above) always returns 0, so there is no way
// to reach the REFUSE arm through `handle_check_refuse`'s own top-level
// call chain; `handle_check_decide` is the same real code, callable
// directly. This mirrors this repo's own `unmount_op.pdx`'s new
// M3-001/M3-002 arms, which likewise take `handle_count` as an explicit
// caller-supplied argument rather than re-querying it, for the identical
// testability reason.
//
// Upstream: design/tooling/volume-tooling-ux.md §5.3, §5.4 (paideia-os).

module HandleCheck = structure {

  // --- handle_check_refuse status codes. ----------------------------------
  pub let HC_PROCEED : u64 = 0
  pub let HC_REFUSE  : u64 = 1

  // --- PFS-15 (paideia-os#2582): the KIND_VOLUME cap slot handle_check_
  //     query + unmount_op's revoke loop both invoke on. .bss sentinel,
  //     default zero -- a future wire from `target_resolve.pdx`'s mount-
  //     table walk (once `KIND_PDXFS_MOUNT_TABLE` grows a per-row volume-
  //     cap-slot query op) writes the resolved slot number here before
  //     `handle_check_refuse` runs. Shared with `src/unmount_op.pdx`'s
  //     revoke sweep via a rip-relative [rip + umnt_vol_cap_slot] load
  //     (same cross-module .bss convention mkfs.pdxfs's volume_device_
  //     slot.pdx uses for its own placeholder). On zero (the only
  //     reachable value today), `handle_check_query` short-circuits to
  //     its M2-era `xor rax, rax; ret` posture rather than issue a cap_
  //     invoke against slot 0, which would surface INVOKE_RESULT_INVALID_
  //     HANDLE (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE != 0) and wrongly refuse every
  //     unmount. ------------------------------------------------------
  pub let mut umnt_vol_cap_slot : u64 = uninit @align(8)

  // --- SC+ ID for cap_invoke (matches mount.pdxfs's own volume_device_
  //     slot.pdx and the frozen SC+ freeze alignment table). ------------
  pub let HC_SC_CAP_INVOKE : u64 = 4

  // --- Op code for VOL_OP_QUERY_HANDLE_COUNT (ordinal 10, see
  //     `src/kernel/core/cap/kind_volume.pdx`). op_arg[63:8] must be 0 for
  //     this op, so the op_arg value handed to cap_invoke is just the op
  //     ordinal itself. ------------------------------------------------
  pub let HC_OP_QUERY_HANDLE_COUNT : u64 = 10

  // ==========================================================================
  // handle_check_query(mount_id) -> u64
  //
  // umount.pdxfs.M2-002 (#5) stub -> PFS-15 (#2582) real-wire-on-provisioned.
  // When [rip + umnt_vol_cap_slot] is nonzero (the day PFS-02 seeds the
  // cap), issues a real `cap_invoke(vol_slot, op_arg = HC_OP_QUERY_
  // HANDLE_COUNT = 10)` via SC+ ID 4 -- the kernel-side body is itself
  // still a stub-return-zero (per `kind_volume.pdx`'s VOL_OP_QUERY_HANDLE_
  // COUNT declaration), but the user-space wire is complete end-to-end.
  // When the slot is zero (the only reachable value today, pending the
  // mount-table -> cap-slot wire described in the module-level sentinel's
  // comment), short-circuits to M2's `xor rax, rax; ret` rather than
  // invoking against an invalid slot. mount_id (rdi) is still accepted
  // for ABI parity; the real body does not read it (the cap slot IS the
  // resolution; mount_id's role is purely diagnostic / audit).
  //
  // Pure leaf at the syscall boundary (rax/rdi/rsi caller-save; SYSCALL
  // preserves callee-save by the amd64 fast-syscall convention). No
  // push/pop needed.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let handle_check_query : (u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{cap} =
    fn (mount_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {cap},
      justification: "umount.pdxfs.M2-002 (#5) + PFS-15 (paideia-os#2582): handle_check_query entry point. mov rax, [rip + umnt_vol_cap_slot]; cmp rax,0; je umnt_hc_query_no_slot -> `xor rax, rax; ret` (the M2 stub posture, kept so the slot==0 case does not fire cap_invoke against an invalid handle). Nonzero slot: mov rdi, rax (slot), mov rsi, 10 (HC_OP_QUERY_HANDLE_COUNT, op_arg[63:8]==0 since this op refuses nonzero high bits per kind_volume.pdx), mov rax, 4 (HC_SC_CAP_INVOKE), syscall; ret -- the kernel's rax IS our return (handle_count on success; INVOKE_RESULT_* sentinels on failure, which main.pdx's handle_check_decide treats as nonzero -> HC_REFUSE, fail-closed). mount_id (rdi) is accepted but unread -- the cap slot is the resolution surface, not the mount_id. Pure leaf at the syscall boundary: rax/rdi/rsi all SysV caller-save, no push/pop needed; SYSCALL preserves callee-save registers under the amd64 fast-syscall convention. No `add reg,[mem]`, no `test`. Labels prefixed umnt_hc_query_.",
      block: {
        mov rax, [rip + umnt_vol_cap_slot];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je umnt_hc_query_no_slot;

        mov rdi, rax;                      // slot
        mov rsi, 10;                       // HC_OP_QUERY_HANDLE_COUNT (op_arg == op; high bits 0)
        mov rax, 4;                        // HC_SC_CAP_INVOKE
        syscall;
        ret;

      umnt_hc_query_no_slot:
        xor rax, rax;                      // M2 stub posture (fail-closed to proceed)
        ret;
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // handle_check_decide(handle_count, force_bit, lazy_bit) -> u64
  //     (HC_PROCEED | HC_REFUSE)
  //
  // umount.pdxfs.M3-001 (#7): the pure refusal-decision leaf, extracted
  // from M2's handle_check_refuse body so it is directly callable with
  // an explicit handle_count (see module header). Refuses iff
  // handle_count > 0 AND force_bit == 0 AND lazy_bit == 0.
  //
  //   rdi = handle_count
  //   rsi = force_bit    0 or nonzero
  //   rdx = lazy_bit     0 or nonzero
  //
  // Pure leaf: touches only rax, no push/pop, no nested call.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let handle_check_decide : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (handle_count: u64) (force_bit: u64) (lazy_bit: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "umount.pdxfs.M3-001 (#7): handle_check_decide entry point. `cmp rdi,0; je umnt_hc_dec_proceed` -- zero handles always proceeds. `cmp rsi,0; jne umnt_hc_dec_proceed` -- nonzero force_bit bypasses. `cmp rdx,0; jne umnt_hc_dec_proceed` -- nonzero lazy_bit ALSO bypasses (unmount_op.pdx's own DEFERRED arm is where the lazy case is actually handled; this gate only decides whether main.pdx refuses up front). Falling through all three sets HC_REFUSE (1); umnt_hc_dec_proceed sets HC_PROCEED (0). Pure leaf -- rax only, no push/pop. No `add reg,[mem]`, no `test`. Labels prefixed umnt_hc_dec_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je umnt_hc_dec_proceed;

        cmp rsi, 0;
        jne umnt_hc_dec_proceed;

        cmp rdx, 0;
        jne umnt_hc_dec_proceed;

        mov rax, 1;                        // HC_REFUSE
        ret;

      umnt_hc_dec_proceed:
        xor rax, rax;                      // HC_PROCEED
        ret;
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // handle_check_refuse(mount_id, force_bit, lazy_bit, out_handle_count_ptr)
  //     -> u64 (HC_PROCEED | HC_REFUSE)
  //
  //   rdi = mount_id             forwarded to handle_check_query verbatim
  //   rsi = force_bit            0 = --force not set; nonzero = --force set
  //   rdx = lazy_bit             0 = --lazy not set; nonzero = --lazy set
  //                              (umount.pdxfs.M3-001, #7 -- see module
  //                              header for why this joins force_bit)
  //   rcx = out_handle_count_ptr u64 write target; ALWAYS written
  //                              (the caller needs the count on BOTH
  //                              outcomes to populate
  //                              PdxFsUnmountRecord@0.1's handle_count
  //                              field even on the HC_PROCEED path)
  //
  // PFS-15 (paideia-os#2582) register plan: before PFS-15,
  // handle_check_query was a pure `xor rax, rax; ret` leaf, so rsi/rdx/
  // rcx all survived the nested call untouched. After PFS-15 the query
  // can issue a SYSCALL on the (today-unreachable) provisioned-slot arm,
  // and the kernel's #743 info-leak hardening zeroes EVERY caller-save
  // register on sysret -- so rsi/rdx/rcx would be clobbered. This file
  // now stages all three into callee-save registers (rbx/r12/r13) BEFORE
  // the query call and restores them from those slots after. The three-
  // register save shape also lands rsp%16==0 at the handle_check_decide
  // call site (entry rsp%16==8; 3 pushes = 24 bytes -> rsp%16==0).
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let handle_check_refuse : (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{cap} =
    fn (mount_id: u64) (force_bit: u64) (lazy_bit: u64) (out_handle_count_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {cap},
      justification: "umount.pdxfs.M2-002 (#5) + M3-001 (#7) + PFS-15 (paideia-os#2582): handle_check_refuse entry point. 3-push callee-save plan (rbx=force_bit, r12=lazy_bit, r13=out_handle_count_ptr). Entry rsp%16==8, 3 pushes 24B -> rsp%16==0 for both nested calls (handle_check_query, handle_check_decide). `call handle_check_query` with rdi already == mount_id (no re-mov needed) -> rax = handle_count (always 0 at this landing via that function's no-slot short-circuit, per PFS-15's module-level sentinel comment); rsi/rdx/rcx may have been zeroed by the kernel's #743 hardening on the (future) provisioned-slot syscall arm, so this file loads them FRESH from rbx/r12/r13 after the call. `mov [r13+0], rax` writes the count out unconditionally. Then `mov rdi, rax` (handle_count), `mov rsi, rbx` (force_bit, restored), `mov rdx, r12` (lazy_bit, restored); `call handle_check_decide` and return its result verbatim. Pops r13/r12/rbx (reverse push order) before `ret`. Given handle_check_query's current no-slot short-circuit always returns 0, HC_REFUSE is unreachable through THIS call chain at this landing -- handle_check_decide itself (module header) is directly testable with a real nonzero handle_count. No `add reg,[mem]`, no `test`. Labels prefixed umnt_hc_ (none needed at this function's size).",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;

        mov rbx, rsi;                      // rbx = force_bit
        mov r12, rdx;                      // r12 = lazy_bit
        mov r13, rcx;                      // r13 = out_handle_count_ptr

        call handle_check_query;           // rdi = mount_id already in place
        mov [r13 + 0], rax;                // *out_handle_count_ptr = handle_count

        mov rdi, rax;                      // handle_count
        mov rsi, rbx;                      // force_bit (restored)
        mov rdx, r12;                      // lazy_bit (restored)
        call handle_check_decide;

        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      }
    }
}
